+      "kind": "concept",
+      "title": "Draft: all-endpoint wandering lower bounds fail for a bounded positive iid law",
+      "statement": "DRAFT, independent review pending. In nearest-neighbor undirected FPP on Z^d, d>=2, with iid Uniform[1,2] edge weights, the unique geodesic from 0 to e1 is the direct edge in every environment: its cost is at most 2, while every other self-avoiding path has odd length at least 3 and cost at least 3. Hence D(0,e1)=0, where D is maximum Euclidean deviation of the geodesic set from the joining segment. For every real r, inf_{v!=0} E D(0,v)/|v|^r=0. This is a finite-endpoint obstruction, not a statement about asymptotic wandering. The all-endpoint lower infimum printed in Auffinger-Damron equation (2.2), https://arxiv.org/pdf/1109.0523, differs from the tail-cutoff lower premise in Chatterjee Theorem 1.1, https://annals.math.princeton.edu/wp-content/uploads/annals-v177-n2-p07-p.pdf. Preserve that distinction when invoking either theorem; changing a cited premise needs justification.",
